+# Definition of Done
+
+> `ToolRegistry.execute` looks a tool up, logs the input's key names, and calls the executor
+> without ever checking the declared `inputSchema` (`src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts`).
+> Every tool declares a JSON Schema (`src/types/ai.ts`) that is handed verbatim to both
+> providers, but nothing validates what comes back from the model.
+
+**Strategy: `direct`** — `ToolRegistry.register`, `ToolRegistry.execute`, and `createChatOrchestrator` already expose the exact seams where schemas are registered, tool inputs are validated before executors run, and rejection responses re-enter the provider tool loop.
+
+## Done when these pass
+
+- [ ] **rejects missing required fields and disallowed enum values while passing valid input with unlisted keys unchanged** — `ToolRegistry.execute` enforces `required` and `enum` without calling the executor, and forwards valid input with unlisted extra keys unchanged without injecting `additionalProperties: false` or schema defaults
+      `src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts`
+- [ ] **rejects a wrong-typed field naming its path and expected type while keeping input values out of results and logs** — `ToolRegistry.execute` rejects wrong-typed fields without coercing types or invoking the executor, names the failing property path and expected type, and omits both offending and identifying input values from `ToolResponse.result` and emitted log lines
+      `src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts`
+- [ ] **compiles a static schema at registration time and throws naming the tool when the schema cannot be compiled** — `ToolRegistry.register` compiles a static `ToolDefinition`'s `inputSchema` up front and throws an error naming the tool on an invalid schema
+      `src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts`
+- [ ] **validates a function-valued tool definition against the schema returned on each execute call** — `ToolRegistry.execute` resolves a function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` on each call and validates against its current `inputSchema` rather than a stale schema from an earlier call
+      `src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts`
+- [ ] **feeds a validation rejection back to the provider as a tool result so a corrected retry resolves the turn** — `createChatOrchestrator` sends a validation rejection's `result` back to the provider as a `tool_result` message rather than throwing, allowing a corrected retry on the next round to resolve `processMessage`
+      `src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts`
+
+## Facets
+
+- [ ] Add `ajv` to `package.json` `dependencies` (with `package-lock.json` updated) so compiled `dist/index.js` imports cleanly for package consumers
+- [ ] Compile static tool schemas once in `ToolRegistry.register` (throwing with the tool name on compilation failure) and cache function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` validators by resolved schema object reference
+- [ ] Validate `input` in `ToolRegistry.execute` before calling `tool.execute`, returning `{result}` and logging only failing property paths and failed constraints (never property values), with a one-line doc comment on `execute` pointing at `specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md`
+- [ ] Add unit tests in `src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts` (including single compilation across ten static `execute` calls and preserving the `Unknown tool: <name>` throw) and `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.test.ts`, link the remaining statements in `specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md`, and advance its status to `Shipped`
+- [ ] Update `README.md`, `docs/adding-a-tool.md`, `.specify/spec.md`, and `CHANGELOG.md` (including the MINOR migration note for executors that previously saw malformed input)
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Changing the `Unknown tool: <name>` throw for unregistered tool names into a returned `ToolResponse`
+- Validating tool output, hallucination detection, or an evaluation harness
+- Per-tool authorization against `ToolContext` or `beforeToolCall` veto hooks
+- Retry, timeout, and rate limiting around tool execution, or concrete tool implementations
